00:44:17,454 --> 00:44:22,112
这样你早上醒来时，就能看到非常漂亮、整洁的代码了。
And so hopefully you wake up in the morning and you've got beautiful clean code to look at.

474
00:44:22,414 --> 00:44:31,152
这就是我试图优化流程的目标，因为我真的很讨厌那种在终端之间不断切换的状况，
And that's what I was trying to optimize my process around because I was really sick of what I and what I still do to an extent of just switching between terminals,

475
00:44:31,438 --> 00:44:34,116
不停地进行上下文切换，就像砰、砰、砰、砰地跳来跳去。
context switching all the time, just going bum, boom, boom, boom, boom, boom, boom.

476
00:44:34,512 --> 00:44:43,312
我想要并试图优化的目标是：完成一大块规划工作，然后让 Agent 工作几个小时。
What I wanted and what I'm trying to optimize for is to just get a good chunk of planning done and then let the agent work for a couple of hours.

477
00:44:43,502 --> 00:44:51,872
这样我就能处理其他工作了，比如每隔 15 分钟专注做一件事，或者去规划别的事情，然后再去审查代码。
And then I can do other work, decent chunks of time, 15-minute chunks working on one thing, planning on stuff, and then I can review the code and do that.

478
00:44:52,334 --> 00:44:55,392
这就是我一直在做 Raphael loops 时一直试图优化的目标。
So that's what I was trying to optimize for all the time when I was doing Ralph loops.

479
00:44:55,472 --> 00:45:01,232
这也是我在 12 月份发现的一件大事：这些家伙已经足够好可以委派任务了，所以我可以让他们在我不操作时继续运行。
And that was the big thing that I found in December is these guys are good enough to delegate to, and so I can run them AFK.

480
00:45:01,552 --> 00:45:05,712
然后你还有一个更具野心的技能，叫做 Wayfinder 技能。
And then you have a different skill as well, which is a bit more ambitious called the Wayfinder skill.

481
00:45:06,032 --> 00:45:06,672
我们可以聊聊这个吗？
Can we talk about that?

482
00:45:06,992 --> 00:45:14,144
当然可以。就像我注意到「执行」这一项需要拆分到多个会话中一样。
Absolutely. So in exactly the same way that Implementation I noticed needed to be split out over multiple sessions.

483
00:45:14,414 --> 00:45:18,384
有时候你在进行高强度对练时，会遇到限制。
Sometimes you're grilling something and you're hitting the limits.

484
00:45:18,862 --> 00:45:26,404
比如你要搞个什么“做一个 Stripe 克隆版”之类的任务，你肯定会碰到限制。在 15 万 标记 的范围内根本没法规划完。
You're going to grill something, you know, build me a Stripe clone or something, right? You are going to hit the limits there. There's no way you can plan that in 150K tokens.

485
00:45:26,766 --> 00:45:34,624
所以我当时就在想，我该怎么拆分它，才能让这些对练环节变成可以无限持续的？
And so I thought, how do I break that up so that I can run grilling sessions that can be infinite length, right?

486
00:45:34,990 --> 00:45:37,892
我该如何拆分对练，好让它能这样运作？
How do I split up grilling so that it can work like that?

487
00:45:38,544 --> 00:45:40,944
于是我就有了这个主意。
And so I came up with this idea.

488
00:45:41,166 --> 00:45:43,396
再次强调，我是在思考信息的流转。
Again, I'm thinking about the flow of information.

489
00:45:43,630 --> 00:45:47,108
本质上是：为了在对练中表现良好，它需要具备什么？
Essentially, like what does it need to perform well in a grilling session?

490
00:45:47,534 --> 00:45:55,184
它可能需要准确理解这次对练的目的，但同时也需要了解目前为止已经达成了哪些共识，
It probably needs to understand exactly what it's uh what the purpose of that grilling session is, but it also needs to understand what's been decided so far,

491
00:45:55,534 --> 00:45:58,852
还需要了解此时此刻可能正在进行的其他对练。
needs to understand what other grilling sessions might be happening at that moment.

492
00:45:59,184 --> 00:46:02,916
于是我提出了“地图”这个概念。
And I came up with this idea of a map.

493
00:46:03,310 --> 00:46:10,064
而这张地图就是所有为了做出决策所需要信息的中心点。
And the map would be the sort of center point of everything that was needed for all the decisions that you were coming up with.

494
00:46:10,382 --> 00:46:19,264
一旦有了地图，你就会意识到，就像在寻找目的地时，有些事情是我必须决定的，
And once you've got a map, you realize, okay, there are certain things I can, like as I'm trying to find my way to a destination, there are certain things I know I need to decide,

495
00:46:19,470 --> 00:46:21,604
这些点就像是地图上的里程碑。
certain points that are kind of like milestones on the map.

496
00:46:21,966 --> 00:46:23,744
而且还有“战争迷雾”。
And there's there's a fog of war.

497
00:46:23,950 --> 00:46:32,304
这个很棒的比喻带我完成了剩余技能的设计。因为有了地图，有战争迷雾，你大概知道要去哪。
And that that lovely metaphor just sort of carried me through designing the rest of the skill, right? Because you've got your map, you've got your fog of war, you vaguely know where you're going.

498
00:46:32,526 --> 00:46:35,824
每一次对练，都会在地图上揭开更多点位。
And every time you have a grilling session, it opens out more points on the map.

499
00:46:36,046 --> 00:46:38,144
这样你就搞清楚了要去哪里。
And so you sort of figure out where you're going.

500
00:46:38,446 --> 00:46:45,024
所以这有点像一个有向无环图，你沿着它走直到到达最终目的地。
And so this is kind of like a directed acyclic graph where you're walking down until you reach your final destination.

501
00:46:45,390 --> 00:46:50,244
有了地图后，其中的每一个独立会话就是地图上的任务单。
And so you've got the map, and then each individual session in there are tickets on that map.

502
00:46:50,670 --> 00:46:54,372
我意识到，对练很好用，但如果你需要做原型设计呢？
And I realized, okay, grilling is good, but what if you need to prototype?

503
00:46:54,544 --> 00:46:59,748
如果你需要做调研呢？或者你需要完成一些随机任务，比如部署一些基础设施之类的？
What if you need to do research? What if you need to do like an arbitrary task, like provision some infrastructure or something?

504
00:47:00,174 --> 00:47:06,224
这些都是地图上不同类型的任务单。而 Wayfinder 基本上就是引导你走过这个过程。
Well, those are different types of tickets on the map. And Wayfinder basically just guides you through this process.

505
00:47:06,542 --> 00:47:11,812
我有的地图包含 50 到 100 个任务单，直到我最终到达目的地。
I've had maps that have, you know, 50, 100 tickets or something until I finally reach my destination.

506
00:47:12,142 --> 00:47:20,064
我实际上也把它用在课程规划上了。也就是非技术类的内容，这非常棒。我甚至用它来规划在花园里建个工作室。
I've actually been using it for course planning as well. So non-technical stuff, which is really great. I've been using it to build a garden office in my garden, right? It's,

507
00:47:20,944 --> 00:47:25,184
你知道，很多时候我们说，这些技能对工程领域很有用。
you know, a lot of these skills, we say, okay, these are great for engineering.

508
00:47:25,550 --> 00:47:31,652
但随后你会发现，工程只是一个领域，我们现在是在做什么？我们只是在讨论一些事情。
Then you realize, okay, engineering is just a discipline that what are we doing here? We're just discussing something.

509
00:47:31,886 --> 00:47:35,908
我们在现实生活中做实事，比如在网站上点来点去之类的。
We're doing things in real life, like clicking around websites and stuff.

510
00:47:36,206 --> 00:47:39,584
你会发现这些逻辑可以轻易地应用到其他领域。
You realize how easily that can map onto other domains.

511
00:47:39,824 --> 00:47:49,024
所以这部分内容，也许我们稍后可以稍微聊聊。我在思考这些东西在不同学科以及不同生活领域中的可迁移性。
So that's kind of maybe we can touch on that a bit later, which is I am thinking how transposable this stuff is into different disciplines and into different areas of life.

512
00:47:49,184 --> 00:47:50,404
Wayfinder 表现得非常出色。
So Wayfinder has been great.

513
00:47:51,182 --> 00:47:59,460
是的，但如果我们思考工程学和软件工程中一个有趣的点，当 Hillel Wayne 参加播客时，他采访了那些他认为是真正工程师的人，比如
Yeah, but if we think one interesting thing about engineering and software engineering, when Hillel Wayne was on the podcast, he interviewed engineers like

514
00:48:00,366 --> 00:48:06,304
化学工程师、机械工程师、土木工程师，并试图弄清楚软件工程是否也是真正的工程。
who he thought are real engineers, chemical engineers, mechanical engineers, civil engineers, and to try to find out is software engineering real engineering.

515
00:48:06,464 --> 00:48:09,092
到最后，他发现它确实是。
And in the end, he found that it probably is.

516
00:48:09,744 --> 00:48:16,676
但他提到，软件工程中一个非常有趣且与其他所有工程领域迥异的点，就是我们所处理的材料。
But he said that one interesting thing with software that is very different to every other engineering profession is the materials that we work with.

517
00:48:16,944 --> 00:48:24,144
在每一个领域，无论是机械工程、土木工程甚至是化学工程，你都有一种具有特定阈值的材料。
In every single place, mechanical engineering, civil engineering, even chemical engineering, you have a material that has a threshold of things.

518
00:48:24,304 --> 00:48:28,772
你并不完全确定它的具体状态，但你知道它能承受大约多少负载等等。
You don't know exactly what it's like, you know that it'll be like it can take about this much load, et cetera.

519
00:48:29,024 --> 00:48:33,904
但在软件中，这种“材料”就是软件，也就是程序本身。
But in software, the material is software which is it it just works like a program.

520
00:48:34,224 --> 00:48:39,184
我的意思是，如果去掉那些非确定性的因素，这或许会更接近传统的工程。
I mean, take out the non-deterministic, which which maybe brings us to more engineering.

521
00:48:39,630 --> 00:48:47,024
但是软件代码，你运行一千次，它就会执行一千次同样的操作，而在其他领域并非如此。
But the software, a code, you run it a thousand times and it does the same thing a thousand times, whereas in other fields it doesn't.

522
00:48:47,246 --> 00:48:49,344
他提到他看到了巨大的差异。
And he said that that he sees a big difference.

523
00:48:49,504 --> 00:48:57,664
但现在我觉得有了大语言模型，我们可能拥有了一种在运行一千次时会产生各种变体的东西，而这正是大多数工程领域所具备的特性。
But now I guess with LLMs, maybe we have this thing where we have a thing where you run a thousand times and it it will have these variants that most of engineering has.

524
00:48:57,744 --> 00:49:04,864
所以谁知道在 LLM 中奏效的方法是否也能用于其他工程领域，毕竟那些领域早就有这些特性了。
So who knows if if both what works with LLMs will be useful at other engineering, where, again, they already had this virus,

525
00:49:05,024 --> 00:49:12,944
或者我们可以从其他工程专业中汲取一些方法，来更好地处理这种被称为 AI 的“材料”。
or or we can take some approaches from other engineering professions that will maybe work nicely with working with this material called AI.

526
00:49:13,264 --> 00:49:13,988
我完全同意。
I totally agree.

527
00:49:14,464 --> 00:49:24,064
我认为软件工程有趣的一点，也是 Agent 处理它很出色的原因是：所有的输入和输出都是基于文本的。
What I think is interesting about software engineering and the way the reason agents are good with it is it's all the inputs and all of the outputs are text-based.

528
00:49:24,928 --> 00:49:31,620
所有的一切。无论是代码、文档，还是给 Agent 下达的操作指令，全都是基于文本的。
Everything. So the inputs, code, documentation, instructions for the agent on what to do, all text-based.

529
00:49:31,854 --> 00:49:39,568
而输出更多是代码、测试套件、类型检查结果、代码检查等，这些全部都是基于文本的。
And the output is more code, is test suites, is type checking results, linting, all that stuff is text-based.

530
00:49:39,758 --> 00:49:43,364
Agent 真正难以处理的是任何非文本类的内容。
The thing that agents really struggle with is anything that's non-text-based.

531
00:49:43,662 --> 00:49:48,612
不过你知道，你看到了一些令人惊叹的演示，人们第一次尝试就直接生成出完美的 UI。
But you know, you see these amazing demos of people one-shotting a perfect UI first time.

532
00:49:48,974 --> 00:49:54,724
那如果那个 UI 有交互问题呢？比如你悬停在某个元素上时动画效果不对？
Well, what about if you have an interaction problem in that UI? What if you're like hovering over something and the animation doesn't look right?

533
00:49:55,008 --> 00:49:56,848
你打算怎么把这个问题传达给 Agent？
How are you going to get that to the agent?

534
00:49:57,008 --> 00:50:05,408
我的意思是，你可以录制一段视频，我想它可能会在某些帧上停顿一下，但就目前的视觉能力来说，这其实并不好用。
I mean, you can record it a video, I suppose, and it sort of pauses on certain frames, let's say, but it's actually not that good in terms of vision just yet.

535
00:50:05,742 --> 00:50:12,164
所以任何非文本内容对 Agent 来说都是垃圾信息。它根本处理不了。
And so anything that's non-text-based is just garbage from the agent. It just can't handle it.

536
00:50:12,528 --> 00:50:19,808
因此我认为在那些领域，如果你能把……我猜他们是在做模拟，对吧？
And so I think in those sorts of professions, if you can turn, I assume they're doing simulations, right?

537
00:50:19,968 --> 00:50:28,368
我猜他们会进行某种……我不确定你有没有那种可以处理架构图的 linter。我相信你们肯定有一些类似的工具，或者某种模拟。
I assume they're doing some kind of, you know, I don't know if you have like a linter that can work on architectural diagram. I'm sure you have some variety of that, right? Some simulation.

538
00:50:28,558 --> 00:50:34,116
如果你能让它变成基于文本的，如果你能把你日常生活中遇到的交互转化成文字，
If you can make that text-based, if you can take the interactions that you have in your day-to-day life and turn them into text,

539
00:50:34,608 --> 00:50:37,728
而这些大部分本来就是文本，那么 Agent 就能做得非常出色。
which mostly they are anyway, then the agents are going to do a pretty good job.

540
00:50:37,998 --> 00:50:45,316
这也是我目前正在尝试做的事情：把所有我使用的服务都接入到 Agent 中，让它们对 Agent 可用。
That's something I'm trying to do currently is take all of the services that I use and plug them into agents, right? Make them available to the agents.

541
00:50:45,728 --> 00:50:46,688
没错。
But yeah.

542
00:50:47,022 --> 00:50:55,648
我们的工作越是符合 Agent 的逻辑，得到的结果就越好。Aaron Powell 回到 AI 整体以及它带来的变化。
The more we can make our work agent-friendly, the better results we're going to get. Aaron Powell Circling back to AI as a whole and then what it's changed.

543
00:50:55,968 --> 00:51:05,968
AI 改变了很多东西。但关于 AI，经常会出现的一个观点——尤其是研究人员和 AI 公司的人常说的——就是“没有先验知识”。
Like it has changed so many things. But one thing that comes up with AI is often, especially researchers and people working in AI companies, is no priors.

544
00:51:06,128 --> 00:51:09,648
有了 AI，你应该抛弃我们之前掌握的一切，因为这玩意儿完全不同。
With AI, you should let go of everything that we know before because this thing is different.

545
00:51:10,030 --> 00:51:15,088
从零开始，以前的方法可能行不通。事实上，就假设它们行不通，去寻找新的方法。
Start from scratch, the approaches might not work. In fact, let's assume they don't work and come up with new approaches.

546
00:51:15,488 --> 00:51:22,372
作为 AI 出现前的开发者，你其实非常热衷于构建高质量、优秀的软件。
Having been a developer before AI, and actually like you're like a like you you were really interested in building quality, great software.

547
00:51:23,168 --> 00:51:28,368
你认为 AI 在多大程度上改变了所有东西，包括基础知识？
How much do you think AI has changed of everything, including the fundamentals?

548
00:51:28,768 --> 00:51:31,300
这也是我一直在思考的问题。
This is something that I thought too.

549
00:51:31,648 --> 00:51:35,248
我想，没错，AI 改变了一切。
I thought, right, AI has changed everything.

550
00:51:35,438 --> 00:51:38,724
我是不是在“倒掉洗澡水的同时把孩子也扔了”？
I'm gonna throw the baby out with the bathwater, right?

551
00:51:38,990 --> 00:51:41,568
我认为我们只需要用全新的视角来看待一切。
I think we just need to look at everything in a new way.

552
00:51:41,870 --> 00:51:46,564
我开始经常这样做。我特别关注的是像「规范驱动开发」这样的概念，也就是
I started doing that a lot. I was especially looking at like spec-driven developments, you know, which is

553
00:51:47,248 --> 00:51:51,652
我对这个词有些复杂的感情，我觉得这个词有点奇怪，它涵盖的内容太多了。
I have sort of mixed feelings towards I think it's a strange term, it encompasses too much.

554
00:51:52,048 --> 00:52:00,132
我想，好吧，也许英语是现在最火的编程语言？Aaron Powell 这段话在某些时候流传开来，因为我把它发到了网上。没错。
And I thought, okay, right, maybe English is the hot new programming language, right? Aaron Powell Which which which went viral at some point when I'm covered to posted it. Exactly.

555
00:52:00,368 --> 00:52:08,768
就像也许我可以只写一个规范，而这个规范是持久存在的。它会成为我可以修改的东西，并让 Agent 在过程中不断去更新它。
Like maybe I can just write a spec, and that specification is going to be persistent. It's going to be something I can edit and just get the agent to change it as it goes.

556
00:52:08,928 --> 00:52:14,928
而在我尝试的过程中，我试了很多次，结果却比我手动编写代码还要糟糕。
And as I experimented with it, I tried it a lot, and I was just getting worse results than if I'd coded it by hand.

557
00:52:15,118 --> 00:52:16,708
而且情况并没有好转。
And it wasn't getting better as well.

558
00:52:17,088 --> 00:52:24,608
我注意到每当我运行这个循环——修改规范、查看代码变化时，代码都会变得更糟。
And I noticed that every time I would sort of run this loop of change the spec, see the code change, the code would get worse.

559
00:52:24,942 --> 00:52:28,516
它本来不该去看代码的，当然，但我看了代码，结果是一堆垃圾。
He's not supposed to look at the code, of course, but I looked at the code and it was garbage.

560
00:52:28,878 --> 00:52:32,128
于是我想，Agent 在这种情况下怎么可能表现良好？它怎么运作呢？
And I thought, how is the agent going to perform well in here? How is it going to work?

561
00:52:32,366 --> 00:52:34,916
因为反馈循环对 Agent 至关重要。
Because the feedback loops are so important to the agent.

562
00:52:35,182 --> 00:52:40,288
如果你有一个糟糕的测试套件，Agent 就会从中得到错误的信号，就像人类一样。
If you have a bad test suite, the agent is going to get bad signal from it, just like a human would.

563
00:52:40,526 --> 00:52:47,648
于是我想，我该如何改进测试套件？我该如何让这个系统不再每次都产出垃圾？
And I thought, how do I improve the test suite? How do I get this setup not not like churning out garbage every time?

564
00:52:47,918 --> 00:52:54,688
然后我打开了一本放在架子上的书，我觉得它第一次拿出来时还裹着塑料膜。
And I just I opened a book that I had on my shelf that I think was still wrapped in plastic the first time I took it out.

565
00:52:55,086 --> 00:53:03,424
那就是《Pragmatic Programmer》。嗯，每个人都叫我读这本书。每个人都说，没错，这是史上最好的书，你一定要读。
Which was the Pragmatic Programmer. Um, which is everyone told me to read it. Everyone, like, you know, everyone said, yeah, this is the best book ever. You just got it.

566
00:53:03,504 --> 00:53:05,316
我买了它，但出于某种原因一直没读。
And I bought it and I didn't read it for some reason.

567
00:53:05,742 --> 00:53:09,584
当我打开它时，里面有一个完整的章节，一个专门关于「软件熵」的章节。
And I opened it and it had a whole chapter, a whole section on software entropy.

568
00:53:09,806 --> 00:53:17,540
而所谓「软件熵」，就是这样一个概念：你知道，熵是指事物倾向于变得更加无序的状态，这比它们进入有序状态的可能性要高。
And software entropy is the concept that, you know, entropy is the idea that things go towards a more disordered state, that that is more likely than them going into an ordered state.

569
00:53:17,904 --> 00:53:20,864
我意识到，好的，软件熵是不可避免的。
And I realized, okay, software entropy is inevitable.

570
00:53:21,038 --> 00:53:25,424
我现在看到的情况是，Agent 产生的软件熵的速度比以往任何时候都要快。
What I'm seeing here is that agents are producing software entropy at a higher rate than ever.

571
00:53:25,646 --> 00:53:27,364
于是我开始深入研究那本书了。
And I started looking more into that book.

572
00:53:27,584 --> 00:53:31,824
而且我读到的几乎每一行，我都觉得，哇，这感觉就像是为现在写的。
And almost every line I read, I thought, wow, this feels like it was written for today.

573
00:53:31,904 --> 00:53:40,384
你知道，你应该去翻翻那本书，这些理念比如「不要跑出你的灯光范围」、始终在反馈循环内工作、偶然编程、追踪子弹……
You know, you should go back to that book, these ideas of like, don't outrun your headlights, always work within your feedback loops, programming by coincidence, tracer bullets,

574
00:53:40,624 --> 00:53:42,544
这么多精妙的观点。
so many smart ideas.

575
00:53:42,894 --> 00:53:45,504
我意识到这本书已经出版 25 年了，对吧？
And I realized this book has been out for 25 years, right?

