I have like a morning stand-up with my agent where I get it, it's it schedules my day for me and like it understands all of my Discord chats and all that.

772
01:10:59,856 --> 01:11:06,976
是的。拥有这种远程连接对我来说感觉要合理得多。我现在唯一在本地做的事情就是调试与远程机器相关的机器人问题。
Yeah. Having that remote feels like it makes just so much more sense for me. And the only thing I do locally now is debugging issues with the remote bot. Yeah.

773
01:11:07,056 --> 01:11:18,016
我想我在想，是否存在如何更轻松地在云端复制一些复杂的本地设置的问题。但一旦这成为可能，那只是时间问题，而不是能不能的问题。
I think I wonder if there's a question of how easiest to replicate some pretty complicated local setups in the cloud. But once that becomes possible, it's probably a matter of when, not an if.

774
01:11:18,350 --> 01:11:22,336
是的。而且如果非要说的话，人们在处理本地设置时也遇到过类似的问题，对吧？
Yeah. And if anything, um people are having this similar issue with local setups, right?

775
01:11:22,576 --> 01:11:26,212
就是成千上万个 Git 工作树塞满了他们的硬盘。
With just a thousand Git work trees just spamming their hard drive.

776
01:11:26,576 --> 01:11:33,536
还有，我该如何建立一个需要运行五个 Docker 容器才能搞定本地开发环境的工作树？
And with how do I have a work tree that I've got to run like five Docker containers in order to get my local dev setup?

777
01:11:33,806 --> 01:11:37,616
嗯，这在云端通常会容易一点，因为你可以按需分配所需的资源。
Well, that's often a little bit easier in the cloud, because you can just provision the resources that you need on demand.

778
01:11:38,016 --> 01:11:48,816
顺便说一下，我们看到像 Ramp、Stripe、Uber 这些拥有平台团队的公司，成功地将开发者的完整本地环境迁移到了云端，
And by the way, we're seeing that companies like Ramp, Stripe, Uber that have platform teams that manage to take a local dev's full setup and put it into the cloud

779
01:11:48,976 --> 01:11:52,740
到一台你可以通过 Slack 或网站就能调用的云端机器上。
on a cloud machine that you can now invoke with this with an at Slack or a website.

780
01:11:53,006 --> 01:12:00,496
他们发现人们在更广泛地使用这些智能体，除了前端工作，因为你仍然需要那种反馈循环。
They're seeing people use these agents far more, except for front-end work, which you still want to have that feedback loop.

781
01:12:00,558 --> 01:12:08,496
就是，有一些例外情况，比如为了降低延迟之类的，你确实需要那种本地开发环境。
that, you know, There are a few exceptions where you really want to have that like a local dev set up for latency or whatnot.

782
01:12:08,656 --> 01:12:13,376
但他们也看到有 70% 到 80% 的开发人员是自愿选择云端的。
But they're also seeing like 70, 80 percent of devs are just voluntarily going for the cloud.

783
01:12:13,710 --> 01:12:13,936
是的。
Yeah.

784
01:12:14,190 --> 01:12:21,924
我的意思是，我觉得你可以通过隧道连接，如果它运行在开发服务器上，并且显示在你本地机器上，
I mean, I I think you can just tunnel through and just get the uh if it's running a dev server and you just have that appearing on your local machine,

785
01:12:22,158 --> 01:12:24,176
那这和在本地运行有什么区别呢？
how is that different from having it locally, right?

786
01:12:24,671 --> 01:12:32,896
好吧。我不确定。我觉得我还没试过那个，但当我提到那个并说“哦，前端可能是个很好的例外”时，这就是我得到的直接反馈。
Okay. I don't know. I think I think I've not experimented with that, but that's when I talked about that and said, oh, maybe front-end is a good exception, that was the immediate response that I got.

787
01:12:33,646 --> 01:12:34,336
这对我来说是合理的。
And it makes sense to me.

788
01:12:34,638 --> 01:12:41,856
我想问你关于规划和需求的问题。你非常相信 Grill Me 以及预先规划，或者获取计划后再让代理去执行。
I want to ask you about planning and requirements. You're a big believer in grill Me and planning and planning up front or getting the plan and then having the agent work.

789
01:12:41,936 --> 01:12:43,536
但这里有一个反方观点。
But there's a devil's advocate here.

790
01:12:43,696 --> 01:12:49,892
代理在执行方面非常快。你甚至可以让好几个代理去实现不同的架构。
Agents are so fast at implementing. You can actually even have like several like a few agents implement different architectures.

791
01:12:50,256 --> 01:12:58,096
那如果采取这种方法呢：既然它们执行很快，我可能不需要做那么多前期规划，我可以边做边纠偏。
What about the approach of like, well, they're they're fast at implementing, so I might not need to do as much upfront planning. I can just course correct as I go.

792
01:12:58,496 --> 01:13:00,976
这取决于你正在做哪种类型的工作，对吧？
It depends what type of work you're doing, right?

793
01:13:01,216 --> 01:13:04,164
因为我认为你不应该把所有事情都用 GrillMe 来处理。
Because I believe that you shouldn't be using GrillMe for everything.

794
01:13:04,336 --> 01:13:13,776
本质上，当你需要处理那些规模很大、且一旦出错很难回溯的任务时，你就需要用到 GrillMe。
Essentially, you need GrillMe for pieces of work where the actual thing being done is going to be quite large and hard to row back from.

795
01:13:14,016 --> 01:13:22,336
如果你觉得，好吧，这个功能可能是一个全新的页面，或者是一个很大的功能，这段代码，你认为如果代理搞错了，
If you feel like, okay, this feature, maybe it's a whole new page, maybe it's a big feature, this code, you think if you if the agent gets it wrong,

796
01:13:22,574 --> 01:13:26,976
那么错误的代码就会留在它的上下文窗口里，影响后续产生的所有内容。
then the wrong code is gonna be in its context window, influencing everything that comes afterwards.

797
01:13:27,246 --> 01:13:33,056
而且事后再去回溯修改并进行对齐，成本会非常高。
And actually going back and editing the stuff afterwards and doing the alignment after the fact is going to be expensive.

798
01:13:33,216 --> 01:13:43,776
而在某些情况下，先进行对齐以解决所有棘手问题——比如你的 JSON cookie 或者你的身份验证 标记——然后再动手做，是很有意义的。
Whereas for those cases, it makes sense to align first to answer all of the tricky questions, like your, you know, your JSON cookie or whatever, your authentication token first and then do it.

799
01:13:43,950 --> 01:13:52,256
但对于某些情况，比如简单的 Bug 修复，或者只是把这个按钮向左移动三个像素，显然不需要在操作前进行对齐。
But for some cases, like simple bug fixes or just like move this button three pixels to the left, it's obvious that you don't need to align before that.

800
01:13:52,336 --> 01:13:56,004
如果你能看出这只是一个五行的改动之类的。
You can see the thing if it's just like a five line change or something.

801
01:13:56,176 --> 01:13:57,764
你可以事后再去对齐。
You can align afterwards.

802
01:13:58,062 --> 01:14:04,416
所以我认为，在可行的情况下，你应该尽可能向后推迟（即先执行再对齐）。
And so that's how I think of it is that where you can, you should shift right as much as possible.

803
01:14:04,496 --> 01:14:12,256
而且实际上，我的视频编辑器里有一些特定的功能，我有一个可以发送反馈的按钮。
And actually, there is actually certain features that I have a little in my video editor, I have a button that I can send feedback to it.

804
01:14:12,336 --> 01:14:17,456
我经常把这个用于非常简单的任务，我发送反馈后，它会进入一个 GitHub issue。
And I often use this for very simple tasks, where I send the feedback, it goes into a GitHub issue.

805
01:14:17,616 --> 01:14:22,016
这会立即被执行代理接收到，并立即开始处理。
This immediately gets picked up by an implementer agent, gets just worked on immediately.

806
01:14:22,254 --> 01:14:24,736
然后代码审查代理会介入并审查代码。
Then a code review agent comes in and reviews the code.

807
01:14:24,942 --> 01:14:30,736
最后，我能看到这个实际问题被修复了，到那时我再进行对齐就行。
And then I at the end, I get to see this actual thing being fixed, and I can do my alignment then.

808
01:14:31,022 --> 01:14:35,524
这种方法对于那些非常容易描述、不需要用 Grill 来反复确认的事情非常奏效。
And that's worked really well for things that are very easy to specify, things that I don't need to grill on.

809
01:14:35,856 --> 01:14:41,508
所以这就是选择题：它是不是小到可以事后再对齐？如果是，就别用 Grill Me。
So that those are the choices. You've got, is it a small enough thing that I can align afterwards? Then don't use grill me.

810
01:14:41,776 --> 01:14:43,296
它是否能包含在一个单一会话中？
Does it fit into a single session?

811
01:14:43,502 --> 01:14:44,804
那就用 Grill me。
Then use grill me.

812
01:14:45,230 --> 01:14:49,696
它跨越多个会话吗？如果我需要对整个过程进行对齐，那就用 Wayfinder。
Does it span multiple sessions? I need to align over the entire thing, then use Wayfinder.

813
01:14:50,016 --> 01:14:59,780
很有意思，因为这和很多科技公司在几年前达成的共识其实没太大区别，也就是基于 PRD，产品参考文档。如果是些琐碎的事情，直接做就行。
Interesting because this is not all that different to where some tech companies landed years before, which is on the PRD, the product reference document. If it's something trivial, just build it.

814
01:15:00,096 --> 01:15:09,776
如果需要团队参与，比如是团队层面的范围。我的意思是，在这里写下来，发给团队，甚至可以 CC 给其他团队，但它不是阻塞点。
If it requires the team, like it's a team level scope. I mean, write up here, do you send it out to the team, maybe CC some other teams, but it's not a blocker.

815
01:15:09,966 --> 01:15:14,016
而如果是更大的事情，那它就是个阻塞点。比如我们需要等待反馈。
And if it's something bigger, then it's a blocker. Like we need to wait for feedback.

816
01:15:14,336 --> 01:15:19,360
基本上，我们表达的方式是这样的：听着，如果是一个月的项目，花上两天时间……
Basically, the way we would say it is like, look, If it's like a one month project, like spend two days,

817
01:15:19,662 --> 01:15:26,320
为了以后节省时间，花一两天去规划并不是坏事。但如果只是一个天的工作量，那就别管了。
like it's it's not a bad thing to spend like one or two days planning it because we're going to save time on it. But if it's a, if it's a one day project, like forget about it.

818
01:15:26,480 --> 01:15:30,160
如果是一个年的项目，我的意思是，我们要干嘛？它应该被拆成更小的任务。
If it's a one year project, I mean, what are we doing? Like we should it it should be smaller one.

819
01:15:30,480 --> 01:15:38,240
没错。而且当我这么说的时候，我听到房间外有人在质疑：我们做的这些听起来不像是瀑布式模型吗？
Totally. And I want to like there's a bit of sort of criticism I hear just from outside the room when you say that, which is that doesn't this sound like waterfall, what we're doing?

820
01:15:38,560 --> 01:15:49,280
当我谈到 Wayfinder 以及构建任何类型的规范时，在进入那个阶段之前，我会进行大量的预先激进原型制作。
When I'm talking about Wayfinder and when I'm doing any kind of like building up any kind of spec, I do a lot of upfront aggressive prototyping before we get there.

821
01:15:49,614 --> 01:15:55,120
这个问题反复出现。有人会说：这不就是瀑布式模型吗？我们这是在退回到 70 年代吗？
That's something that comes up again and again and again. It's like, this is just waterfall. What are we doing going back to the 70s?

822
01:15:55,600 --> 01:15:59,280
但是 AI Agent 给你的能力是能大量产出低质量内容，对吧？
But agents give you this ability of just churning out slop, right?

823
01:15:59,566 --> 01:16:03,280
有时候你可以利用这一点，因为原型嘛，对吧？
And sometimes you can use that to your advantage because a prototype, right?

824
01:16:03,502 --> 01:16:11,364
只是为了搞清楚它应该长什么样。你可以做出三个或四个不同的版本，然后选一个你最喜欢的去迭代，不断地、不断地产出。
Just getting a sense for what it should look like, you can build out three or four different versions and just choose your favorite and iterate on it and just keep churning, churning, churning.

825
01:16:11,918 --> 01:16:18,480
这是一种我们以前从未拥有过的非常强大的模式，对吧？以前制作原型总是很昂贵的。
That can be a really powerful setup that we've not really had before, right? It was always expensive to produce prototypes.

826
01:16:18,734 --> 01:16:23,840
现在它成了史上最廉价的。而对我来说，写规范的一个核心部分，其实就是产出这些原型。
Now it's the cheapest that it's ever been. And that's an essential part of writing specs to me, is actually producing these prototypes.

827
01:16:24,240 --> 01:16:27,652
是的，但关于瀑布式的批评，我觉得 Grady Booch 可能也……
Yeah, but also like with the waterfall criticism, I think Grady Booch might have

828
01:16:28,302 --> 01:16:37,280
他也跟我说过这一点。就像是，我们不应该去批评瀑布式模型，因为比如很多大型科技公司，像亚马逊、
told me this as well. It's like, don't forget, like, like we should not criticize waterfall because for example, a lot of big tech, the largest tech companies from like Amazon,

829
01:16:37,440 --> 01:16:42,080
微软、谷歌、Meta，随便你挑，他们其实都在做微型瀑布式模型。
Microsoft, Google, Meta, you name it, they are kind of doing mini waterfall.

830
01:16:42,160 --> 01:16:47,396
在 AI 出现之前，他们一直在做这种微型瀑布：制定计划、达成共识、开始构建、然后发布。
Like pre-AI, they've been doing pretty mini waterfall, which is let's do a plan, let's agree on it, let's build it, let's ship it.

831
01:16:47,630 --> 01:16:51,600
而这一切都是在两周、一个月、两个月或三个月内完成的。
And this is all done in like two weeks, a month, two months, three months.

832
01:16:51,886 --> 01:16:53,040
三个月算是极端情况了。
Three months is kind of the extreme.

833
01:16:53,486 --> 01:16:55,920
但 Grady Booch 说，瀑布式模型的问题从来不是这个。
But Krady Booch was saying the problem was never this with waterfall.

834
01:16:56,000 --> 01:17:02,880
瀑布式模型的问题在于规划过程真的要花一整年，甚至更久，然后实施又要花三年。
The problem with waterfall was the planning was literally taking like a year, like one year, and then the implementation taking three years.

835
01:17:03,150 --> 01:17:07,520
等到四年后准备就绪时，它已经不是我们想要的东西了。那才是问题所在。
And by the time it was ready four years later, it's not what we wanted. And that was the problem.

836
01:17:07,600 --> 01:17:15,840
问题不在于用瀑布式模型去做一个或两个月、或者一周的项目。问题始终是我们在讨论跨度为数年的项目。
It was like the problem is not like having like a one or two month project or a one week project with a waterfall. The problem was always this we're talking years.

837
01:17:16,046 --> 01:17:20,320
他提到，这个行业已经几十年来没见过真正的瀑布式模型了。
And he said that the industry has not seen waterfalls for decades now.

838
01:17:20,558 --> 01:17:29,520
所以我们现在用的这个词，有点像是我们在批评微型瀑布式模型。而这种模式本身并不一定是坏事。你明白我的意思吗？
And so here we're using this term, which is a bit like we're criticizing or mini waterfalls, we're criticizing that one. It's actually that's not a bad thing necessarily. You see what I mean?

839
01:17:30,880 --> 01:17:35,120
它就像是一个被我们攻击的稻草人。没错，它是个已经不存在的东西。
It's kind of a scarecrow that we're punching or something. Yeah, it's a pinata which which stopped existing.

840
01:17:35,440 --> 01:17:43,680
它可能存在于某些我们不知道的、受监管行业的疯狂企业项目中，但我感觉即使在那儿，它大概也已经过时了。
It might exist in some crazy like enterprise projects that none of us know about in regulated industries, but I feel even there it's probably kind of out of style.

841
01:17:43,920 --> 01:17:49,120
是的，我觉得如果我们要去“敲这个皮纳塔”，把它放在那儿其实挺有用的。
Yeah, I think it's like if we're hitting the pinata, I think it's actually a useful thing to have up there.

842
01:17:49,422 --> 01:17:53,636
它就像一个有用的……幽灵，或者说是一个有用的警示故事，对吧？
It's like a useful uh ghost or useful uh cautionary tale, right?

843
01:17:54,160 --> 01:17:54,628
因为
Because

844
01:17:55,566 --> 01:17:59,440
哪一个更符合 Agent 的架构？
what which one fits the agentic setup more closely?

845
01:17:59,630 --> 01:18:06,160
它应该是敏捷的，对吧？因为人力成本大幅降低，我们可以非常、非常快速地做出更改。
It's going to be agile, right? Because the cost of labor has gone down so much, we can just make changes very, very quickly.

846
01:18:06,400 --> 01:18:11,760
我不确定。我觉得这个比喻挺合适的。所以我并不介意去吐槽瀑布式模型，尽管现在已经没人真的用它了。
I don't know. that feels like the right metaphor to me. So I don't mind hating on waterfall, even though no one really does it anymore.

847
01:18:12,240 --> 01:18:18,180
嗯，还有一件事也过时了。我们并不讨厌它，但就是测试驱动开发，TDD。
Well, one other thing that just it just w went out of style. We didn't hate it, but test-driven development, TDD.

848
01:18:18,480 --> 01:18:27,680
你对在 Agent 相关任务中使用 TDD 有什么看法？我和 Ken Beck 交流时，我们讨论过这在很多方面都非常契合，但我还是没看到大家真正去用它。
What is your take on using them for agenda stuff? When I talked with Ken Beck, we talked about how this could be a great fit for many reasons, but I still don't see people really using it.

849
01:18:28,080 --> 01:18:29,188
我看到人们在写测试。
I see people writing tests.

850
01:18:29,280 --> 01:18:33,252
Agent 也会在事后写测试，这也是大多数人的做法。
The agents also write tests after the fact, which is how most people work.

851
01:18:33,550 --> 01:18:35,280
但我记得你一直是 TDD 的倡导者，对吧？
But I think you've been an advocate for TDD, right?

852
01:18:35,600 --> 01:18:43,600
是的。所以我有一项 TDD 技能，我建议使用它。这挺及时的，因为我一直在思考这个问题，但还没正式发布过。
Yeah. So I have a TDD skill, which I recommend using. And this is quite timely because I have been thinking about it, but I haven't really posted about it yet.

853
01:18:43,854 --> 01:18:46,880
TDD 是为了优化极小的工作记忆。
TDD optimizes for having a very small working memory.

854
01:18:47,566 --> 01:18:51,360
对吧？你写一个测试，而那个测试本该失败。
Right? You write one test, and that test is supposed to fail.

855
01:18:51,566 --> 01:18:58,080
这意味着即使你分心了，去喝杯咖啡或者去散个步，当你回来时，测试依然是失败的，
And it means that even if you get distracted, you go for a coffee or something, you go for a long walk, when you come back, the test is still failing,

856
01:18:58,240 --> 01:19:01,920
它能提醒你目前实现到哪一步了，并引导你去做下一步。
reminding you of where you are in the implementation and guiding you to the next thing.

857
01:19:02,094 --> 01:19:03,440
Agent 不需要这个。
Agents don't need that.

858
01:19:03,694 --> 01:19:10,000
对于 Agent 来说，它们很棒的一点是，它们比人类拥有大得多的工作记忆，对吧？
Agents, the thing that's great about agents is that they have a much larger working memory than humans, right?

859
01:19:10,414 --> 01:19:15,332
它们实际上能在大脑中承载比人类目前多得多的信息，这非常有用。
They can actually hold a lot more in their heads than humans can currently, which is very useful.

860
01:19:16,000 --> 01:19:18,916
但它们的并不具备无限的工作记忆。
But they don't have an infinite working memory.

861
01:19:19,120 --> 01:19:24,068
而且我认为 TDD 针对的并不是核心问题。
And TDD, it's sort of aiming at the wrong problem, I think.

862
01:19:24,320 --> 01:19:30,080
但是 Agent 真正需要的是拥有反馈循环。
But the thing that agents really do need is that they need to have feedback loops.

863
01:19:30,222 --> 01:19:35,360
它们需要看到自己在做什么，以及这些操作如何与代码环境交互。
So they need to see what they're doing and how it's interacting with the environment of the code.

864
